Perhaps you have heard of the sand mandalas created by Tibetan monks?
Over days or weeks, they painstakingly create an intricate and beautiful 
pattern out of millions of grains of coloured sand.

Then, to remind themselves of the principle of impermanence, they sweep 
it all up and throw it into the water.

Nethack is a lot like that.  You spend hours hunched over the keyboard, 
fingers flying, and then all of a sudden, your fingers stop, your mouth 
hangs open, you fall back into your chair, stunned at the demise of your 
now-beloved character; the wand of death, the poisoned spikes, most 
likely your own stupidity that brought the whole intricate, beautiful, 
all-consuming proccess to a sudden and irrevocable halt. 

And so you start another game.  "For the sake of us all..."

In any case, what you are doing is called "save-scumming".  That should 
indicate to you the general feeling about it.  As many others will point 
out, once you stop save-scumming, you will begin to learn how to play 
Nethack.

